<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>FIR No. 141/2022 was registered at PS Hauz Khas on 28 February 2022, initially under Sections 302/34 IPC with Arms Act offences; the chargesheet added Sections 201 and 120B IPC. The prosecution traces the enmity to a 2016 murder case in the same police station. After a fresh altercation in 2022, the accused allegedly gathered at a co-accused's premises, and the deceased was shot outside. The applicant was named as one of three main shooters; a pistol linked to him was recovered, and the trial court recorded that ballistic evidence matched the cartridges.</p>

<h2>What had changed by November 2026</h2>
<p>The applicant's first bail application had been dismissed by the Additional Sessions Judge, Saket, on 28 August 2026. Before the High Court, the decisive features were different in kind:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Custody:</strong> in judicial custody since 1 March 2022 — over 4 years and 8 months, with interim bail availed two or three times and no misuse alleged.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Trial pace:</strong> charges framed, 43 witnesses cited, but only six public witnesses examined in four and a half years.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Evidence:</strong> PW-1 to PW-5 — including the cited eyewitnesses — did not support the prosecution. PW-6 claimed to identify the applicant from CCTV footage, but admitted in cross-examination that he was not an eyewitness and is himself an accused in the 2016 FIR.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Parity:</strong> three co-accused had been granted bail by the trial court, which itself relied on the non-support of PW-1 to PW-5.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Court's reasoning</h2>
<p>Justice Sachin Datta anchored the order in the settled proposition that bail is the rule and jail the exception, citing the Supreme Court's recent line of authority including <em>Syed Iftikhar Andrabi v. NIA</em>, 2026 INSC 503, <em>Manish Sisodia v. Directorate of Enforcement</em>, 2024 INSC 595, and <em>Union of India v. K.A. Najeeb</em>, (2021) 3 SCC 713, along with <em>Prabhakar Tewari v. State of U.P.</em>, (2020) 11 SCC 648, for the point that the gravity of the offence and pendency of other cases cannot, by themselves, justify refusal.</p>
<p>The Court was careful on the merits. It said the material against the applicant — particularly PW-6's identification — "cannot be trivialised", and it expressly declined to examine the depositions. But the inconsistencies and the non-support of the cited eyewitnesses could not be ignored at the prima facie stage, and the trial was plainly not concluding any time soon. The combination of prolonged custody, glacial trial progress and the state of the evidence carried the application.</p>
